Adaptation: internal/compute/cuda/gdn_decode_vllm.cuh, included by
internal/compute/cuda/qwen35_gdn.cuh. The 256-thread, four-chunk, paired-warp
reduction and ordered 1..8-token recurrent update are ported directly.
Activations, outputs, and carried state use FAK FP32 rather than upstream BF16
activation/output rounding. FAK's canonical [head,key,value] state is staged
through a shared-memory transpose for upstream's [value,key] computation and
written back coalesced without a persistent layout migration. Shared V is read
directly to keep the FP32 candidate below the default 48 KiB shared limit.
The copy pipeline uses cp.async on SM80+ and a synchronous vector copy when
compiled for older CUDA architectures. Head ratios 1, 2, 3, 4 and 8 are retained.

FAK supplies one in-place carried state and contiguous ordered token panels
for the ordinary path. Independent lanes instead use a bounded by-value table
of up to eight distinct carried-state pointers, one token per lane, and a
two-dimensional value-head/lane grid. They share the retained arithmetic and
weights, not carried state or a time axis.
The upstream accepted-token slot table, per-token destination snapshots,
BF16/FP16 dispatch, sigmoid-only output gate, and PyTorch wrapper are omitted.
The output activation is SiLU, matching FAK's Qwen GDN contract. Existing
convolution, projections, strict device-only ownership, error drain and final
operation fence remain in the FAK caller.

Selection: FAK_CUDA_GDN_POST_CONV=vllm for eligible ordinary decode, ordered
sequence panels of 1..8 tokens, or independent batches of 1..8 one-token lanes
with 128-dimensional key and value heads.
The default, other shapes, longer sequences and graph capture use the existing
native implementation. Kernel profiling names the selected candidate
k_qwen35_gdn_post_conv_vllm for both ordinary and independent-lane
specializations; this shared row alone does not distinguish them.

Verification: TestCUDAGDNVLLMPostConvHost executes this exact source with a
bounded CPU SIMT model against an independent FP64 recurrence, and
TestCUDAGDNVLLMPostConvContract checks selection and caller wiring. The added
TestCUDAGDNVLLMLanesHost carries separate FP64 states across two steps with
distinct lane inputs and guarded outputs; TestCUDAGDNVLLMLanesContract checks
the independent-lane selection and wiring. These host/source witnesses do
not establish CUDA compilation, GPU execution, or performance, and the new
lane witnesses have not yet been executed as part of this source-only port.
The prepared required-device witness is:
FAK_CUDA_GDN_REQUIRED=1 go test -tags cuda ./internal/compute -run '^TestCUDAGDNVLLMPostConvDevice$' -count=1 -v
CUDA compilation, physical state/output parity, whole-model quality and net
performance remain separate qualification requirements. No speed or default
promotion claim accompanies this port.
